{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-42258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89049547bc2ffed2ec35f0c0204dfa13ffa0ed66549f6a44d4756c843dc4eabb"} {"question" "q-42258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}55 \\\\ -21 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-68 \\\\ -22 \\\\ 50\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- $AC+BC=(A+B)C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42265" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89049547bc2ffed2ec35f0c0204dfa13ffa0ed66549f6a44d4756c843dc4eabb"} {"question" "q-42265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-42265" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $3$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89049547bc2ffed2ec35f0c0204dfa13ffa0ed66549f6a44d4756c843dc4eabb"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -9 & 24 & -21\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -7 & -21 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130313" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & -9 \\\\ -8 & 8 & 24 \\\\ 7 & -7 & -21\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- ${A}^{2}B=A(AB)$\n- $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- $B(CA)=(CB)A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  ${A}^{2}B=AAB=A(AB)$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false.  Matrix multiplication is distributive and  $A(B-C)=AB-AC.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AC\\ne CA$ in general and therefore $A(B-C)\\ne AB-CA$, in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $B(CA)=(BC)A.$ However, matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $BC\\ne CB$ in general and therefore $B(CA)\\ne (CB)A$, in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "89049547bc2ffed2ec35f0c0204dfa13ffa0ed66549f6a44d4756c843dc4eabb"} {"question" "q-130446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-130446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-319938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-319938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-65 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032506/state.json" "file_sha256" "a539a32a8dd95d0912b2245a29a49169ae75be19b878410dfc591b197b2c603c"} {"question" "q-319938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty